// Challenge versions (about C1) of the Unit 2 and Unit 3 texts: a fourth level after Easier, Core and Harder.
// Same paragraph letters and facts as the other levels, and one more question for each of the eight slots,
// in slot order. A short-answer question with no groups uses the Harder groups, plus any `extra` groups.
// Students reach Challenge only inside a text, after three right first time in a row on Harder.
const Q = (stem, opts, hint, focus, more = {}) => ({ stem, opts, hint, focus, ...more });
const S = (stem, need, hint, focus, more = {}) => ({ type: "short", stem, need, hint, focus, ...more });

export const CHALLENGE = {
/* ---------- Unit 2 ---------- */
sleep: { level: { title: "Out of sync: the science of the teenage lie-in", paras: [
  ["A", "It is 6.30 on a Monday morning, and Maya's alarm has just gone off for the third time. She was in bed by ten the previous night, yet sleep eluded her until well after midnight. Her parents have long since settled on a verdict: their daughter is lazy, and the remedy is self-evident. Such a verdict has the appeal of simplicity, and it is delivered daily in households the world over. The trouble, as sleep researchers have been pointing out for decades, is that it rests on a fundamental misreading of adolescent biology."],
  ["B", "At the heart of the matter lies the circadian rhythm, the internal clock that governs the daily ebb and flow of drowsiness and alertness. One of its principal levers is melatonin, a hormone whose evening release signals to the body that it is time to wind down. With the onset of puberty, this rhythm is pushed back, and melatonin may not be released until as much as two hours later than in a child or an adult. A fifteen-year-old ordered to bed at ten is therefore being asked to do something their body is not yet ready for, and may well lie awake until eleven or beyond, regardless of willpower."],
  ["C", "The cost of this mismatch is far from trivial. Adolescents are thought to need between eight and ten hours of sleep a night; most, however, make do with around seven, and a sizeable minority with considerably less. Because the shortfall is cumulative, its effects are insidious rather than dramatic: attention wavers and newly learned material fails to stick; mood becomes volatile, with irritability and anxiety never far away; and an immune system starved of rest offers weaker protection against infection."],
  ["D", "Into this already precarious situation comes the smartphone. The blue light from screens inhibits the production of melatonin still further, in effect persuading the brain that dusk has yet to arrive. At the same time, an endless supply of messages and clips keeps the mind in a state of low-level arousal, so that the intention to watch “just one more video” is seldom honoured."],
  ["E", "Faced with this evidence, a growing number of schools have chosen to work with adolescent biology rather than against it. When schools in one American city pushed their start time back to around 8.45, researchers recorded an average gain of roughly half an hour of sleep a night, accompanied by an improvement in grades. Alongside such institutional change, specialists advocate modest adjustments at home: keeping phones out of the bedroom, seeking out morning daylight, which helps to reset the internal clock, and resisting the urge to sleep until midday at weekends."],
  ["F", "None of this absolves teenagers of all responsibility; habits matter, and some are within their control. What the science does undermine is the reflex that equates tiredness with idleness. The caricature of the slothful adolescent reveals less about young people than about the adults who cling to it, and the more useful response is not reproach but a willingness to adjust expectations to the realities of a body in transition."]],
  gloss: "insidious: harmful in a slow, hidden way · reproach: blame or criticism" },
 q: [
  Q("Which of the following best captures the central claim of the article?", ["The common view of tired teenagers as lazy misreads a biological shift, and adults should adjust their expectations.", "Teenagers' sleep problems would disappear if every school started later.", "Smartphones, rather than biology, are the main cause of adolescent sleep loss.", "Teenagers bear no responsibility for their sleep habits because their bodies are to blame."],
    "Compare the parents' verdict in A with the conclusion in F. Reject answers that describe only one paragraph or make an absolute claim.", "adjust expectations to the realities of a body in transition"),
  Q("According to paragraph B, why might a fifteen-year-old sent to bed at ten lie awake?", ["Their body has not yet released the hormone that prepares it for sleep.", "They lack the willpower to switch off their phone.", "Their body releases melatonin two hours earlier than an adult's.", "They have had too much daylight during the day."],
    "Paragraph B: what does melatonin signal, and when does it arrive during puberty?", "being asked to do something their body is not yet ready for"),
  Q("In paragraph C, the effects of sleep loss are “insidious rather than dramatic”. This means they…", ["build up gradually and are easy to overlook", "are sudden and alarming", "are mainly emotional", "affect only a small minority"],
    "Read the words just before: “Because the shortfall is cumulative”. If harm builds up slowly, is it easy to notice?", "its effects are insidious rather than dramatic"),
  S("Paragraph C lists several consequences of the shortfall. Identify two, in your own words.", 2,
    "After the colon in paragraph C, the consequences are separated by semicolons.", "attention wavers and newly learned material fails to stick"),
  Q("What does the phrase “the remedy is self-evident” suggest about Maya's parents?", ["They think the solution is so obvious that it needs no further thought.", "They have asked experts for advice about Maya's sleep.", "They suspect that Maya has a medical problem.", "They are unsure how to help their daughter."],
    "Paragraph A: “self-evident” means obvious without needing proof. How does the writer judge their verdict at the end of the paragraph?", "the remedy is self-evident"),
  S("Explain the two ways in which smartphones make the situation in paragraphs B and C worse.", 2,
    "Paragraph D: one effect is chemical (light); the other is introduced by “At the same time”.", "The blue light from screens inhibits the production of melatonin still further"),
  Q("What role does the American study play in the writer's argument?", ["It provides evidence that adapting to adolescent biology brings measurable benefits.", "It shows that the problem is limited to the United States.", "It suggests that habits at home matter more than school timetables.", "It introduces a view that the writer goes on to challenge."],
    "Paragraph E opens with “Faced with this evidence…”. What do the study's results show about working “with adolescent biology”?", "researchers recorded an average gain of roughly half an hour of sleep a night"),
  Q("Which statement most accurately reflects the writer's position in paragraph F?", ["Teenagers are partly responsible for their habits, but treating their tiredness as laziness is unjustified.", "Teenagers should not be expected to change any of their habits.", "Adults alone are to blame for teenagers' poor sleep.", "Science proves that teenagers are never lazy."],
    "Paragraph F begins with a concession (“None of this absolves…”) and then turns. Avoid answers with “alone”, “never” or “any”.", "None of this absolves teenagers of all responsibility")
 ] },

food: { level: { title: "Hidden hunger in an age of plenty", paras: [
  ["A", "Ask most people to picture malnutrition and they will describe the emaciated children of famine photographs. The picture is not false, but it is dangerously partial. In clinical terms, malnutrition denotes any diet that fails to meet the body's nutritional needs, and the definition therefore extends well beyond hunger to include those who consume too much of the wrong things. Seen in this light, the condition is not confined to the world's poorest regions; in one form or another, it is almost everywhere."],
  ["B", "That is not to play down the scale of hunger itself. Hundreds of millions of people still lack sufficient food, and the vast majority of them are poor. The damage done to children is especially lasting: undernourished children are more likely to suffer stunted growth, to succumb to infection and to fall behind at school, setbacks that can shape the rest of their lives."],
  ["C", "At the opposite extreme, more than a billion people are now living with obesity. Diets built around fast food and junk food account for a good deal of this. Such foods are dense in calories, largely thanks to fat and sugar, but nutritionally thin, supplying few of the vitamins and minerals the body depends on. Sustained over years, obesity markedly raises the likelihood of chronic conditions such as type 2 diabetes and heart disease."],
  ["D", "What confounds the conventional picture is that these apparent opposites so often occur side by side, at times under a single roof. In many cities, a burger and a fizzy drink cost less than a meal of fresh vegetables and fish, and households on tight budgets are, in effect, steered towards food that keeps hunger at bay cheaply while contributing little to health. Hence the paradox at the centre of the issue: it is entirely possible to be overweight and malnourished at once."],
  ["E", "Waste gives the problem an especially exasperating twist. Roughly a fifth of the food that reaches consumers in shops and homes is thrown away, much of it still perfectly fit to eat. In response, a growing number of governments either oblige or incentivise supermarkets to pass unsold food on to charities instead of consigning it to landfill."],
  ["F", "Experts refer to this combination as the “double burden” of malnutrition, and no single policy will lift it. Individual choices are not irrelevant: beans and lentils are cheap and nutritious, and one hardly needs to be vegetarian to enjoy them, while eating less junk food and wasting less are obvious places to begin. Yet to place the burden of change on consumers alone would be to mistake the symptom for the cause. As long as a healthy diet remains a luxury, calls to eat well will ring hollow for those least able to afford to follow them."]],
  gloss: "emaciated: extremely thin because of hunger · ring hollow: sound insincere or useless" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best summarises the article as a whole?", ["Malnutrition covers both hunger and obesity, which are often linked by the high cost of healthy food.", "Obesity has replaced hunger as the world's main nutritional problem.", "Malnutrition would largely disappear if consumers made better choices.", "Food waste is the root cause of both hunger and obesity."],
    "A summary must include both halves of the “double burden” and the link in paragraph D. Paragraph F rejects one of these options directly.", "the definition therefore extends well beyond hunger"),
  Q("According to paragraph E, how have some governments responded to food waste?", ["By requiring or rewarding supermarkets for giving unsold food to charities.", "By banning supermarkets from throwing any food away.", "By taxing consumers who waste food at home.", "By building more landfill sites for food."],
    "Paragraph E: “oblige” means force; “incentivise” means encourage with a reward. Which option includes both?", "oblige or incentivise supermarkets to pass unsold food on to charities"),
  Q("In paragraph C, foods described as “nutritionally thin” are…", ["low in the vitamins and minerals the body needs", "low in calories", "likely to make people lose weight", "made without fat or sugar"],
    "Read the rest of the sentence in paragraph C: what do these foods supply “few of”?", "but nutritionally thin", { para: "C" }),
  S("Paragraph F mentions some individual choices that could help. Identify two.", 2,
    "Paragraph F: the sentence that begins “Individual choices are not irrelevant”.", "eating less junk food and wasting less are obvious places to begin"),
  Q("What does the expression “in effect, steered towards” imply about low-income households?", ["Their diets are shaped by prices rather than freely chosen.", "Supermarkets deliberately mislead poor customers.", "They prefer junk food to fresh vegetables.", "Governments tell them what to buy."],
    "Paragraph D: who or what is doing the steering? Look at the price comparison just before.", "households on tight budgets are, in effect, steered towards food"),
  S("Explain how it is possible to be overweight and malnourished at once.", 2,
    "Combine paragraph D (why families buy this food) with paragraph C (what the food contains and lacks).", "it is entirely possible to be overweight and malnourished at once"),
  Q("Why does the writer describe the familiar picture of malnutrition as “dangerously partial”?", ["To accept that it is true while warning that it hides a large part of the problem.", "To argue that famine photographs are deliberately misleading.", "To suggest that hunger is no longer a serious issue.", "To criticise doctors for defining malnutrition too broadly."],
    "Paragraph A: “not false, but…”. What does the rest of the paragraph add to the familiar picture?", "The picture is not false, but it is dangerously partial."),
  Q("Which statement best reflects the writer's view in paragraph F?", ["Personal choices help, but the main obstacle is that healthy food is unaffordable for many.", "Consumers are chiefly responsible for solving the double burden.", "Individual choices make no difference to malnutrition.", "Charities should take full responsibility for feeding the poor."],
    "Paragraph F: note “not irrelevant” and then “Yet”. What does the writer see as the real cause?", "to place the burden of change on consumers alone would be to mistake the symptom for the cause")
 ] },

anger: { level: { title: "The anatomy of a temper", paras: [
  ["A", "Ninety seconds from the end of the school football final, with his side a goal down, Khoa was bundled off the ball. The referee, unmoved, waved play on, and in that instant something in Khoa gave way. He charged across the pitch to protest, and before he had fully registered what he was doing, he was making the long walk to the touchline, a red card held aloft behind him. “I felt awful,” he admits. “I'd let the whole team down at the worst possible moment.”"],
  ["B", "It would be easy to draw the moral that anger is simply a liability, but psychologists warn against that conclusion. Anger is a universal emotion and by no means an inherently destructive one; at its best, it alerts us to injustice and supplies the energy to confront it. The real problem lies not in feeling anger at all, but in what we allow ourselves to do while it has us in its grip."],
  ["C", "Part of what makes anger so treacherous is its sheer speed. Within seconds, the heart begins to race and the bloodstream is flooded with adrenaline, a hormone that readies the muscles for a fight. At the same time, activity is suppressed in the regions of the brain that govern judgement and forward planning. It is hardly surprising, then, that so many of the things people say and do in anger are later bitterly regretted."],
  ["D", "Fortunately, self-control is better understood as a skill than as an inborn trait, and like any skill it can be practised. Slow, deep breathing works against the body's alarm response, while counting to ten buys a crucial pause between impulse and action. A subtler technique is to label the emotion, telling yourself quietly, “I'm feeling angry.” Brain-imaging studies indicate that putting a feeling into words in this way can measurably blunt its force. Where circumstances allow, simply walking away for a few minutes gives the body a chance to recover its equilibrium."],
  ["E", "Empathy, the ability to recognise and share another person's feelings, provides a second line of defence. Faced with someone whose behaviour grates, pausing to consider what pressures they might be under can alter our reaction entirely. This is not to say that bad behaviour should be excused; rather, empathy can stop a passing irritation from escalating into outright hostility."],
  ["F", "None of this implies that young people should be taught to suppress their competitive instincts, which are a powerful spur to effort and improvement. The argument is rather that competitiveness realises its value only when it is harnessed by self-control: a composed player is an asset to any team, whereas a talented but volatile one is a liability. Khoa now runs through a breathing routine before every match. “I still get angry,” he says. “The difference is that I'm in charge now, not my anger.”"]],
  gloss: "equilibrium: a calm, balanced state · volatile: likely to change mood suddenly" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best captures the central argument of the article?", ["Anger is a natural and sometimes useful emotion; the harm comes from acting on it without control.", "Anger is a liability that young people should learn to suppress completely.", "Competitive sport makes young people more likely to lose their temper.", "Brain-imaging research has found a cure for anger."],
    "Paragraph B rejects one “easy” moral. What does it put in its place? Check that your answer also fits D, E and F.", "The real problem lies not in feeling anger at all"),
  Q("According to paragraph C, what happens in the brain when we become angry?", ["Activity drops in the areas responsible for judgement and planning.", "The brain releases adrenaline to calm the muscles.", "Activity increases in the areas that control planning.", "The brain slows the heart to prepare for a fight."],
    "Paragraph C: find “At the same time”. Is brain activity increased or reduced, and in which regions?", "activity is suppressed in the regions of the brain that govern judgement and forward planning"),
  Q("In paragraph D, putting a feeling into words can “blunt its force”. This means it can…", ["make the feeling less intense", "make the feeling sharper", "hide the feeling from others", "make the feeling last longer"],
    "A blunt knife does not cut well. Does naming the emotion make anger stronger or weaker?", "can measurably blunt its force"),
  S("Paragraph D describes several techniques for managing anger. Identify two, in your own words.", 2,
    "Paragraph D: breathing, counting, labelling the emotion and walking away.", "simply walking away for a few minutes gives the body a chance to recover its equilibrium",
    { extra: [{ label: "name the feeling", k: ["label", "name", "words", "say"] }] }),
  Q("What does “before he had fully registered what he was doing” suggest about Khoa's reaction?", ["It was an impulse that came before conscious thought.", "He had planned to protest from the start of the match.", "He did not hear the referee's whistle.", "He was pretending to be angrier than he was."],
    "Paragraph A: if you act before you “register” it, were you thinking it through? Paragraph C explains why this happens.", "before he had fully registered what he was doing"),
  S("Explain the physical and mental changes that lead people to do things in anger that they later regret.", 2,
    "Paragraph C: one change is in the body (heart, adrenaline); the other is in the brain (judgement and planning).", "Within seconds, the heart begins to race and the bloodstream is flooded with adrenaline"),
  Q("Why does the writer return to Khoa at the end of the article?", ["To show the advice working in practice, completing the story begun in paragraph A.", "To suggest that Khoa no longer feels anger at all.", "To criticise the referee's decision in the final.", "To introduce a new argument about competitive sport."],
    "Compare Khoa in A and F. Does he still feel angry? What has changed?", "Khoa now runs through a breathing routine before every match.", { para: "F" }),
  Q("Which statement best reflects the writer's view of competitiveness?", ["It is worthwhile, but it is only fully valuable when it is controlled.", "It should be discouraged because it causes anger.", "It matters more than self-control in sport.", "It is valuable only for talented players."],
    "Paragraph F: “None of this implies…” and “The argument is rather that…”.", "competitiveness realises its value only when it is harnessed by self-control")
 ] },

germs: { level: { title: "The war you never notice", paras: [
  ["A", "Your throat is raw, your nose will not stop running and every muscle aches: you have a cold. Yet while you lie in bed nursing your misery, a remarkably sophisticated campaign is under way inside you. Your immune system has already launched a coordinated response to the infection, and, barring complications, it will have the upper hand within a week or so."],
  ["B", "Most infections can be traced to one of two kinds of microorganism: bacteria or viruses. Bacteria are single living cells, so tiny that thousands could sit on a full stop. Viruses are smaller again and, strictly speaking, incapable of reproducing independently; in order to multiply, a virus must break into one of your cells and commandeer its machinery to churn out copies of itself. Both the common cold and flu fall into this second category."],
  ["C", "The body defends itself in layers. Skin acts as a physical barrier; mucus in the nose traps invaders before they can penetrate further; and stomach acid destroys a great many of the germs we swallow. Any that breach these outer defences are tracked down and attacked by white blood cells. Even fever, for all the discomfort it causes, is part of the counter-offensive, because a raised temperature makes it harder for many pathogens to reproduce."],
  ["D", "A stubborn misconception holds that antibiotics will cure a cold. In fact, antibiotics act on bacteria and are wholly ineffective against viruses. Prescribing them where they are not needed is not merely futile but actively harmful: it hastens the spread of antibiotic resistance, as the bacteria that survive adapt, so that these drugs risk failing in exactly the situations where they matter most."],
  ["E", "Nor is it accurate to cast bacteria as uniformly hostile. The human body is home to trillions of them, roughly as many as there are human cells, and the overwhelming majority are either harmless or positively useful. Bacteria in the gut assist with digestion, and some appear to play a role in calibrating the immune system, effectively training it to tell real threats apart from harmless substances."],
  ["F", "So what genuinely speeds recovery? Rest, sufficient sleep and plenty of fluids; regular hand-washing, meanwhile, protects the people around you. The immune system is the product of millions of years of evolutionary refinement, and in most cases the wisest course of action is the least dramatic one: to step aside and let it do its job."]],
  gloss: "commandeer: take control of something that belongs to someone else · futile: useless" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best captures the overall message of the article?", ["The body's layered defences usually overcome common infections, so recovery depends more on supporting them than on medicine.", "Antibiotics should be the first treatment for most infections.", "Bacteria are the body's main enemy and should be destroyed wherever possible.", "Fever is a dangerous symptom that must always be reduced."],
    "Paragraph A introduces the “campaign” and paragraph F gives the conclusion. Which option fits both and is contradicted by none of B to E?", "Your immune system has already launched a coordinated response to the infection"),
  Q("According to paragraph B, what distinguishes viruses from bacteria?", ["They cannot multiply without taking over a host cell.", "They are larger than bacteria.", "They are single living cells.", "They cause most stomach infections."],
    "Paragraph B: find “strictly speaking”. What can a virus not do on its own?", "incapable of reproducing independently"),
  Q("In paragraph E, the writer warns against casting bacteria as “uniformly hostile”. This means seeing them as…", ["all equally harmful", "harmful only in large numbers", "completely harmless", "harmful only to the immune system"],
    "“Uniformly” means in the same way every time. What does the rest of paragraph E say about most bacteria?", "Nor is it accurate to cast bacteria as uniformly hostile.", { para: "E" }),
  S("Identify two layers of the body's defence against infection.", 2,
    "Paragraph C works from the outside in: skin, nose, stomach, then the blood.", "Any that breach these outer defences are tracked down and attacked by white blood cells."),
  Q("What point is the writer making with the words “for all the discomfort it causes”?", ["Fever feels unpleasant but actually helps to fight infection.", "Fever is the most painful symptom of a cold.", "Fever should be treated quickly to reduce discomfort.", "Fever is a sign that the immune system has failed."],
    "Paragraph C: “for all” here means “despite”. What role does the writer give to fever?", "Even fever, for all the discomfort it causes, is part of the counter-offensive", { para: "C" }),
  S("Using paragraphs B and D, explain why antibiotics will not cure a cold.", 2,
    "Paragraph B: what kind of germ causes a cold? Paragraph D: what do antibiotics act on?", "antibiotics act on bacteria and are wholly ineffective against viruses"),
  Q("How does paragraph E develop the argument of paragraph D?", ["It gives a further reason not to treat all bacteria as enemies to be wiped out.", "It explains how bacteria become resistant to antibiotics.", "It challenges the warning about the overuse of antibiotics.", "It describes a disease caused by bacteria in the gut."],
    "Paragraph E begins with “Nor”, which adds a second, similar point. What is the first point, in D?", "the overwhelming majority are either harmless or positively useful"),
  Q("Which statement best reflects the writer's view in paragraph F?", ["For common infections, the best approach is usually to support the body and let it recover.", "Medicine is never needed to treat an infection.", "The immune system can defeat every disease unaided.", "Hand-washing matters more than rest for recovery."],
    "Paragraph F: “in most cases”. Avoid options with “never” or “every”.", "the wisest course of action is the least dramatic one")
 ] },

/* ---------- Unit 3 ---------- */
crowds: { level: { title: "When places are loved to death", paras: [
  ["A", "On a summer morning in Venice, the lanes around St Mark's Square can become so congested that walking turns into shuffling. A substantial proportion of the crowd consists of day-trippers, many of them poured out of cruise ships, who photograph the canals, buy a souvenir and are gone by nightfall. For those who actually live there, the picture looks very different. Spiralling rents and permanently crowded streets have driven tens of thousands of residents out of the historic centre in recent decades. “Our city is turning into a museum,” remarks one local woman, and the observation is anything but affectionate."],
  ["B", "The phenomenon has acquired a name, overtourism: the point at which the number of visitors outstrips what a destination can reasonably absorb. Venice is by no means an isolated case; Rome, Barcelona and Dubrovnik have all grappled with the same dilemma. Sightseeing may rank among the great pleasures of travel, but when everyone converges on the same sight at the same moment, the experience is diminished for visitors and residents alike."],
  ["C", "Historic sites are especially exposed. The Colosseum, whose tiers of stone arches have withstood almost two thousand years, now admits millions of visitors annually. The relentless tread of their feet is wearing down its steps, and a number of visitors cannot resist running their hands over ancient surfaces. Indoors, medieval and Renaissance frescoes face a less visible danger: the breath and body heat of dense crowds raise humidity levels, slowly degrading pigments that have endured for centuries."],
  ["D", "Authorities have begun to take action. In 2024, Venice imposed an entry fee on day-trippers on its busiest days. In Milan, visitors to Leonardo da Vinci's The Last Supper are let in in small groups for a strictly enforced fifteen minutes. Increasingly, sites depend on timed tickets and sensors that track visitor numbers in real time, allowing them to close their doors before crowding becomes damaging."],
  ["E", "Yet the reverse problem may be no less pressing. While a handful of celebrated destinations buckle under the strain, countless lesser-known sites can scarcely attract visitors at all. Deprived of tourist income, they fall prey to neglect: leaking roofs go unrepaired and walls gradually crumble. Given that tourism pays for a considerable share of conservation work, the task is not so much to reduce the number of travellers as to spread them more evenly."],
  ["F", "None of this amounts to an argument for shunning famous places altogether. Travelling outside the peak season, staying longer rather than rushing through, and venturing beyond the obvious destinations may each seem a modest choice, but in aggregate they make a real difference. What is ultimately at stake is coexistence: a balance in which those who live in remarkable places and those who travel to admire them can both continue to do so."]],
  gloss: "converge: come together in one place · in aggregate: all added together" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best captures the article's overall argument?", ["Tourism is concentrated in too few places, harming some sites and starving others, so visitors need to be spread more evenly.", "Overtourism can only be solved by charging every visitor an entry fee.", "Tourists should avoid famous sites altogether.", "Historic sites suffer mainly from a lack of visitors."],
    "The article describes two opposite problems (A to D, then E). The best summary covers both, and the writer's aim in F.", "What is ultimately at stake is coexistence"),
  Q("According to paragraph D, what do sensors allow sites to do?", ["Close before crowding causes damage.", "Charge visitors for the time they stay.", "Identify which visitors came by cruise ship.", "Plan next year's conservation work."],
    "Paragraph D, last sentence: what does tracking visitor numbers in real time allow?", "allowing them to close their doors before crowding becomes damaging"),
  Q("In paragraph E, celebrated destinations “buckle under the strain”. This means they…", ["cannot cope with the pressure of so many visitors", "become even more famous", "raise their prices", "close for repairs"],
    "Something that buckles bends or collapses under a heavy weight. What is the “strain” here?", "buckle under the strain"),
  S("Identify two ways in which large crowds damage historic sites.", 2,
    "Paragraph C: feet, hands, and what breath and body heat do to the air.", "slowly degrading pigments that have endured for centuries"),
  Q("What does “anything but affectionate” suggest about the local woman's remark?", ["She regrets that the city is becoming a place to look at rather than to live in.", "She is proud that Venice has so many museums.", "She is joking about tourists and their photographs.", "She thinks museums should charge an entry fee."],
    "“Anything but” means “not at all”. Think about what has happened to residents earlier in paragraph A.", "the observation is anything but affectionate"),
  S("Explain how a shortage of visitors can lead to damage at lesser-known sites.", 2,
    "Paragraph E: what are these sites deprived of, and what then happens to their roofs and walls?", "Deprived of tourist income, they fall prey to neglect"),
  Q("What is the function of paragraph E within the article?", ["It complicates the argument with the opposite problem, which shapes the writer's proposed solution.", "It gives further examples of ways to control crowds.", "It argues that famous sites should close to visitors.", "It contradicts the claim that tourism damages historic sites."],
    "Paragraph E opens with “Yet”. How does it change the solution suggested at the end of the paragraph?", "Yet the reverse problem may be no less pressing.", { para: "E" }),
  Q("Which statement best reflects the writer's position in paragraph F?", ["Individual travel choices seem small but, taken together, can make a real difference.", "Tourists are entirely to blame for damage to historic sites.", "Only strict government limits can solve overtourism.", "Famous destinations should be avoided completely."],
    "Paragraph F: “in aggregate” means taken together. Avoid answers with “entirely”, “only” or “completely”.", "in aggregate they make a real difference")
 ] },

camping: { level: { title: "Off the grid, against my will", paras: [
  ["A", "When my parents announced that we would be spending the weekend camping in a national park, my reaction was, to put it charitably, muted. My younger brother, Bao, had spent weeks lobbying for a theme park, and I had been quietly looking forward to a weekend at home with my phone. “You do realise there's no Wi-Fi,” Mum added, with a smile that suggested she was enjoying this rather more than I was. I resigned myself to what promised to be the longest forty-eight hours of my life."],
  ["B", "The park lies some two hours from our city, and we were booked into what is advertised as an eco campsite: no shop, no plastic bottles, and a strict limit of twelve tents, which keeps it remarkably peaceful. A ranger met us at the gate and went through the rules with the gravity of someone who has watched every one of them being broken: every scrap of rubbish leaves with you, fires are lit only in the designated area, music stops at nine, and under no circumstances do you feed the wildlife."],
  ["C", "On the first morning we set off for a waterfall, Dad in charge of the picnic basket and I of the first-aid kit, which, mercifully, never needed opening. For all the heat outside, the forest beneath the canopy was cool and almost eerily quiet. Halfway up, we were scrutinised by a troop of monkeys, who watched us from the branches with an air of faint disapproval. Bao was so entranced that the theme park was never mentioned again."],
  ["D", "At dusk, clouds of mosquitoes rose from the riverbank. Mum, it turned out, had packed insect repellent, so we applied it generously and changed into long sleeves. We emerged without a single bite, although the slapping and muttering from the neighbouring tent suggested that its occupants had been rather less well prepared."],
  ["E", "The real highlight came on the second afternoon, in a natural hot spring where water heated far below ground wells up to the surface. We sat in it as the sun slid behind the hills. The ranger, who had joined us, said something that has stuck with me ever since: “People come here to enjoy nature, but if they're careless, they destroy the very thing they came to see. Eco tourism means leaving a place better than you found it.”"],
  ["F", "I went two full days without so much as glancing at my phone and, much to my irritation, did not miss it in the slightest. I have since been campaigning for a longer trip next year. A day at a theme park is entertaining while it lasts; a weekend in a national park, I have had to concede, is something you carry home with you."]],
  gloss: "muted: not strong or enthusiastic · concede: admit, often unwillingly" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best captures what the text is about?", ["A reluctant teenager is won over by a weekend of eco camping that leaves a lasting impression.", "A teenager argues that families should ban phones on holiday.", "A family learns how to deal with dangerous wildlife.", "A writer compares the advantages of theme parks and national parks."],
    "Compare the writer's attitude in A with the last sentence of F. A summary must cover the change.", "is something you carry home with you"),
  Q("According to paragraph B, what makes the campsite so peaceful?", ["It allows only a small number of tents.", "It has no shop and no plastic bottles.", "It is two hours from the city.", "A ranger patrols it at night."],
    "Paragraph B: find “peaceful”. Which detail does “which keeps it…” refer back to?", "a strict limit of twelve tents, which keeps it remarkably peaceful"),
  Q("In paragraph C, the monkeys “scrutinised” the family. This means they…", ["looked at them closely and carefully", "ran away from them", "made loud noises at them", "tried to take their food"],
    "Read the rest of the sentence in paragraph C: how did the monkeys watch them, and with what expression?", "we were scrutinised by a troop of monkeys", { para: "C" }),
  S("Identify two of the rules that visitors to the campsite must follow.", 2,
    "Paragraph B: the rules come after the colon that follows “broken”.", "under no circumstances do you feed the wildlife"),
  Q("What does the ranger's “gravity” suggest about the campsite rules?", ["They are taken seriously because visitors have often ignored them.", "The ranger enjoys telling visitors what to do.", "The rules have recently been changed.", "The family had broken the rules on an earlier visit."],
    "“Gravity” means seriousness. Why would someone who has watched the rules being broken explain them so seriously?", "with the gravity of someone who has watched every one of them being broken", { para: "B" }),
  S("Explain why the family emerged without a bite while their neighbours did not.", 2,
    "Paragraph D: what rose from the riverbank, and what had Mum packed?", "Mum, it turned out, had packed insect repellent"),
  Q("Why does the writer quote the ranger at the end of paragraph E?", ["To leave the reader with the central message about responsible tourism.", "To show that the ranger was annoyed with the family.", "To explain how the hot spring was formed.", "To suggest that eco tourism is impossible."],
    "The writer says the words have “stuck”. What do they teach every visitor?", "said something that has stuck with me ever since"),
  Q("Which phrase best describes the writer's tone in paragraph F?", ["Wryly honest about having been proved wrong", "Bitter about being made to give up the phone", "Sad and nostalgic that the weekend is over", "Dismissive of people who prefer theme parks"],
    "Paragraph F: why would it be “irritating” not to miss the phone? Is the writer really upset, or laughing at themselves?", "much to my irritation, did not miss it in the slightest")
 ] },

active: { level: { title: "Learning to fall", paras: [
  ["A", "Fourteen-year-old Minh tightened his grip on the tow rope as the boat surged forward. For three exhilarating seconds he was skimming across the surface of the lake; then, without warning, he was underwater. A cheer went up from the boat. “I fell eleven times that day,” he recalls with a grin. “But on the twelfth attempt, I made it all the way round.”"],
  ["B", "Minh was spending a fortnight at a summer sports camp, and he is part of a growing trend. Travel companies report a steady rise in demand for active holidays, as families exchange sunbeds and theme-park queues for windsurfing, waterskiing, rock climbing or, rather incongruously, ice skating at an indoor rink at the height of summer."],
  ["C", "The attraction goes well beyond physical fitness. Psychologists observe that mastering a demanding skill, particularly one that involves repeated failure, builds a resilient kind of confidence that carries over into other spheres of life. Because campers spend their days in groups, friendships are forged quickly, and screen time plummets, almost without anyone noticing."],
  ["D", "At Minh's camp, scarcely an hour went unaccounted for. Mornings were given over to windsurfing on the lake, while afternoons alternated between matches on the tennis court and roller skating through the local park. By evening, Minh says, “everyone was too exhausted to even glance at their phones.”"],
  ["E", "None of this is entirely free of risk. Falls and minor injuries are an accepted part of the learning process, which is precisely why reputable camps insist on staff with first-aid qualifications and check the weather forecast every morning. When a storm swept in one afternoon, windsurfing was cancelled without a moment's hesitation and the campers were moved indoors for table tennis. “Nobody argued,” Minh admits. “The lake looked genuinely terrifying.”"],
  ["F", "Active holidays are not for everyone; for some people, a holiday is precisely a chance to do nothing whatsoever, and there is nothing wrong with that. But for those prepared to tolerate a measure of discomfort, they offer something a sunbed seldom can: the satisfaction of returning home able to do something you could not do before. Or, as Minh puts it, “Falling eleven times is fine, as long as you get up twelve.”"]],
  gloss: "incongruously: in a way that seems strange or out of place · resilient: able to recover quickly from difficulties" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best captures the article as a whole?", ["Active holidays are increasingly popular because they bring benefits beyond fitness, as long as the risks are managed.", "Active holidays are too dangerous for teenagers.", "Families should give up relaxing holidays for sports camps.", "The main benefit of active holidays is that they keep children off their phones."],
    "A summary needs the trend (B), the benefits (C), the risks (E) and the writer's balanced view in F.", "he is part of a growing trend"),
  Q("According to paragraph D, how was a typical day at the camp organised?", ["Windsurfing in the morning, then either tennis or roller skating in the afternoon.", "Tennis in the morning and windsurfing in the afternoon.", "Free time in the morning and organised sport in the afternoon.", "Windsurfing all day unless the weather was bad."],
    "Paragraph D: what were mornings “given over to”? What does “alternated” tell you about the afternoons?", "afternoons alternated between matches on the tennis court and roller skating through the local park"),
  Q("In paragraph C, confidence that “carries over into other spheres of life”…", ["also helps in areas outside sport", "stays limited to the sport that was learned", "is lost when the holiday ends", "depends on other people"],
    "A “sphere” here is an area of life. Does the confidence stay at the camp?", "carries over into other spheres of life"),
  S("Identify two benefits of active holidays, apart from physical fitness.", 2,
    "Paragraph C: “The attraction goes well beyond physical fitness.” Look for confidence, friendships and screens.", "friendships are forged quickly, and screen time plummets"),
  Q("What does the detail “recalls with a grin” suggest about Minh's attitude to his failures?", ["He sees falling as a normal, even enjoyable, part of learning.", "He is embarrassed by how often he fell.", "He believes he was simply unlucky.", "He blames the instructors for his falls."],
    "Would someone embarrassed by eleven falls grin? Compare his words at the end of paragraph F.", "he recalls with a grin"),
  S("Explain why reputable camps insist on staff with first-aid qualifications.", 2,
    "Paragraph E: what is “an accepted part of the learning process”? Link it to the staff.", "which is precisely why reputable camps insist on staff with first-aid qualifications"),
  Q("What does Minh's comment “Nobody argued” contribute to paragraph E?", ["It confirms that cancelling was obviously sensible, even to the campers.", "It suggests the campers were too afraid to complain.", "It shows that the instructors were very strict.", "It hints that the campers were disappointed with the camp."],
    "Read the next sentence: what did the lake look like? What does that say about the decision?", "“Nobody argued,” Minh admits."),
  Q("Which statement best reflects the writer's view in paragraph F?", ["Active holidays offer a special satisfaction, though a restful holiday is just as valid.", "Doing nothing on holiday is a waste of time.", "Active holidays are only worthwhile if you master a skill.", "Everyone should be willing to be uncomfortable on holiday."],
    "Paragraph F: the writer makes a concession before “But”. Avoid answers that are too strong.", "and there is nothing wrong with that")
 ] },

monkeys: { level: { title: "The monkeys who discovered the spa", paras: [
  ["A", "High in the mountains of central Japan, the temperature has dropped to minus ten degrees and the forest lies smothered in snow. Yet in a pool of steaming water, a troop of monkeys sits motionless, eyes half closed, seemingly oblivious to the snowflakes settling on their heads. They could easily be mistaken for guests at a luxury spa."],
  ["B", "These are Japanese macaques, popularly known as snow monkeys, and their home is the Jigokudani valley, where water heated deep underground rises to the surface. Japan has thousands of such hot springs, and bathing in them is a treasured part of the country's culture. These macaques, however, are the only monkeys in the world known to bathe in them as a matter of habit."],
  ["C", "The behaviour seems to have begun in the 1960s, when a young female waded into a hot spring beside a local inn to retrieve food that had fallen into the water. Her younger companions copied her, and within a few years the practice had spread throughout the troop, a vivid illustration of how new behaviours can pass between animals. When a park was established in 1964, staff built the monkeys a pool of their own, not least to keep them out of the inn's own baths."],
  ["D", "For decades, the purpose of the habit remained largely a matter of conjecture. In 2018, however, researchers measured levels of a stress hormone in the monkeys' droppings and found that, during the harshest months of winter, females that bathed had markedly lower levels than those that stayed out. The baths, it appears, are less a luxury than a survival strategy, helping the animals to conserve body heat and withstand the rigours of winter."],
  ["E", "Today the monkeys attract visitors from across the globe. Coaches arrive daily, and many tourists make a day trip from Tokyo, trudging up the snowy path beneath a sea of umbrellas. The park enforces strict rules: feeding and touching the animals are forbidden, a distance of a few metres must be maintained, and visitors are warned not to stare into the monkeys' eyes, a gesture the animals may read as a challenge."],
  ["F", "Some researchers are concerned that ever-growing crowds could gradually alter the monkeys' natural behaviour, and the troop is closely monitored. For now, though, the valley offers a rare and precarious model of coexistence, one that rests entirely on visitors accepting that they are guests in the monkeys' home, and not the other way round."]],
  gloss: "conjecture: a guess without proof · rigours: hard and unpleasant conditions" },
 q: [
  Q("Which statement best captures the article as a whole?", ["A learned bathing habit helps the monkeys survive winter and draws tourists, whose presence must be carefully managed.", "Tourism has already destroyed the monkeys' natural behaviour.", "Scientists have shown that hot springs reduce stress in all animals.", "The monkeys bathe mainly to entertain visitors."],
    "A summary needs the habit (C), its purpose (D) and the visitors (E and F). Reject answers that go further than the evidence.", "a rare and precarious model of coexistence"),
  Q("According to paragraph C, how did the bathing habit spread?", ["Younger monkeys copied a young female, and the practice passed through the troop.", "Park staff trained the monkeys to use the pool.", "The oldest monkeys taught the habit to the young.", "The monkeys followed tourists into the water."],
    "Paragraph C: who went into the water first, and who copied her?", "Her younger companions copied her"),
  Q("In paragraph D, the purpose of the habit was “a matter of conjecture”. This means people…", ["could only guess at the reason", "had proved the reason", "were not interested in the reason", "disagreed strongly about the reason"],
    "Read on: “In 2018, however, researchers measured…”. Before that, was there any evidence?", "remained largely a matter of conjecture", { para: "D" }),
  S("Identify two rules that visitors to the park must follow.", 2,
    "Paragraph E: what is forbidden, what must be maintained, and what are visitors warned not to do?", "feeding and touching the animals are forbidden, a distance of a few metres must be maintained"),
  Q("What does “not least to keep them out of the inn's own baths” imply?", ["The monkeys had been using the inn's baths, which the inn did not welcome.", "The inn wanted to attract monkeys to its baths.", "The monkeys were afraid of the inn's baths.", "Visitors complained that the monkeys' pool was too small."],
    "Why would anyone need to keep monkeys out of a bath? “Not least” introduces an important reason.", "not least to keep them out of the inn's own baths"),
  S("Explain why the writer describes the baths as “a survival strategy”.", 2,
    "Paragraph D: what did the bathing females have less of, and what do the baths help them conserve?", "helping the animals to conserve body heat and withstand the rigours of winter"),
  Q("What is the effect of the final sentence of paragraph A?", ["The comparison makes the scene surprising and amusing, and draws the reader in.", "It shows the writer's disapproval of luxury tourism.", "It suggests that the monkeys are kept in captivity.", "It states the article's main argument."],
    "How would a reader react to monkeys described as spa guests? Is this an argument, or a way to catch the reader's interest?", "They could easily be mistaken for guests at a luxury spa."),
  Q("Which statement best reflects the writer's view in paragraph F?", ["Coexistence works for now but is fragile, and depends on visitors respecting the monkeys.", "Tourism will inevitably destroy the monkeys' natural behaviour.", "The monkeys should be moved somewhere without visitors.", "Visitors have every right to interact closely with the monkeys."],
    "Paragraph F: “For now” and “precarious”. Avoid answers that are too strong.", "one that rests entirely on visitors accepting that they are guests in the monkeys' home")
 ] }
};
